3. FActScore's annotation release: obtained, not redistributed
--------------------------------------------------------------

Until 2026-08-04 this repository committed datasets/InstructGPT.jsonl, the
FActScore human-annotation release. It no longer does. The file is obtained from
its authors like every other source, datasets/ is gitignored, and
tools/fetch_factscore.py gives the download route and verifies the result.

Contents, for anyone deciding whether they need it: 183 people, each with a
biography generated by OpenAI's InstructGPT in response to "Tell me a bio of
<person>", broken into 1,123 annotated sentences and 4,726 human-labelled atomic
facts (2,100 supported, 1,971 not supported, 655 irrelevant), plus a rarity and
region bucket per person. Distributed by its authors through a Google Drive link
in the README of github.com/shmsw25/FActScore, as data/labeled/InstructGPT.jsonl.
MIT licensed.

The checksum guard remains, and matters more now than when the file was
committed. The FActScore builder keys each instance by LINE NUMBER, and the
siblings ChatGPT.jsonl and PerplexityAI.jsonl share the schema exactly -- so a
substitution, or a re-download in a different order, yields 330 plausible
instances joined to entirely different biographies with nothing raising.
raim/tasks.py pins FACTSCORE_SHA256 against the copy the reported verdicts were
produced against, and the builder refuses any other file unless
FACTSCORE_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED is set.

Nothing is lost by not shipping it: the authoritative record of which instances
were used is dataset_index/factscore.jsonl, which is committed, content-addressed
and verifiable against any copy of the source.


4. Judged models
----------------

The ten panel members, the frontier and scaling judges, and the purpose-trained
judges are each obtained from their own publishers under their own licences,
which this repository neither includes nor alters. Two panel members are gated
on Hugging Face and require accepting their licences before a run. Model weights
are never redistributed here; only the verdicts they produced.

Judgments obtained through paid APIs (e.g. Anthropic) are subject to those
providers' terms as they stood at the time of the runs.


